Now I see something interesting. `xmlHashFree` calls `f(iter->payload, iter->name)` for each entry. The callback is `xmlTextReaderFreeIDTableEntry` which calls `xmlFreeID`. Inside `xmlFreeID`, it does `DICT_FREE(id->value)` and `DICT_FREE(id->name)`.

But wait - `xmlHashFree` also frees `iter->name` after calling `f`. So if `xmlFreeID` sets `id->name` to point to `iter->name` (which it does in `xmlTextReaderRemoveID`), then `xmlFreeID`'s `DICT_FREE(id->name)` would free `iter->name`, and then `xmlHashFree` would also try to free `iter->name` again!

Wait, let me re-read `xmlFreeID`:

```c
static void
xmlFreeID(xmlIDPtr id) {
    xmlDictPtr dict = NULL;

    if (id == NULL) return;

    if (id->doc != NULL)
        dict = id->doc->dict;

    if (id->value != NULL)
        DICT_FREE(id->value)
    if (id->name != NULL)
        DICT_FREE(id->name)
    xmlFree(id);
}
```

And `DICT_FREE`:
```c
#define DICT_FREE(str)                                          \
        if ((str) && ((!dict) ||                                \
            (xmlDictOwns(dict, (const xmlChar *)(str)) == 0)))  \
            xmlFree((char *)(str));
```

So `DICT_FREE` only frees the string if the dict does NOT own it. If the dict owns it, it doesn't free it. Then `xmlHashFree` also checks `if (table->dict == NULL)` before freeing `iter->name`. So if the hash table has a dict, it doesn't free the name either.

